# MH_HANDLER_ID intentionally omitted — each pod auto-generates a unique ID
# from HOSTNAME + UUID suffix (see config.rs), avoiding conflicts across replicas
# gRPC server for MC communication
MH_GRPC_BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:50053
# Health/metrics endpoint
MH_HEALTH_BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:8083
# WebTransport server for media streams
MH_WEBTRANSPORT_BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:4434
# Deployment region (local for Kind)
MH_REGION=local
# GC service discovery via Kubernetes DNS (registration endpoint)
GC_GRPC_URL=http://gc-service.dark-tower:50051
# AC service discovery for OAuth token acquisition
AC_ENDPOINT=http://ac-service.dark-tower:8082
# AC JWKS endpoint for JWT validation
AC_JWKS_URL=http://ac-service.dark-tower:8082/.well-known/jwks.json
# RETIRED — PENDING REMOVAL. Do not tune, do not "fix", do not read.
#
# Superseded by the egress-budget chain further down this file: MH derives its
# stream ceiling from `MH_EGRESS_BUDGET_BPS` and the two stream-cost keys, and
# that derived value is what it advertises to GC in the `max_streams` field.
#
# WHY IT IS STILL HERE (two-deploy retirement, story 2 R-19/R-24). The MH code
# task deletes the code read, `Config::max_streams` and `DEFAULT_MAX_STREAMS`.
# This key and BOTH `configMapKeyRef` entries (mh-0-deployment.yaml,
# mh-1-deployment.yaml) deliberately survive that deploy: the read goes one
# deploy before the key. This ConfigMap is content-addressed (ADR-0038 §2), so
# `kubectl rollout undo` returns to the PREVIOUS hash-suffixed generation, which
# holds every key its revision references. Undo safety therefore rests on that
# generation being retained (nothing in this tree prunes generations), not on
# key ordering. The ordering protects a partial revert or apply, in which the
# ConfigMap and the Deployments do not move together, and a future move to a
# stable ConfigMap name (docs/runbooks/mh-deployment.md §Config-failure
# triage; corrected 2026-09-30 from the pre-ADR-0038 "undo never reverts the
# ConfigMap" premise).
#
# WHAT IS TRUE NOW (story 2 task 8). NOTHING READS THIS KEY. mh-service
# advertises its DERIVED stream ceiling to GC as `max_streams` (from
# `register()` and `attempt_reregistration()` in
# crates/mh-service/src/grpc/gc_client.rs); editing this value changes
# nothing. (Before that image, the value below was what GC placed against.)
# Enforced by: nothing.
# Where an operator sees it: nowhere — watch `mh_media_egress_stream_ceiling`
# and the startup log's `egress_stream_ceiling` instead.
#
# Scheduled removal, with its own two-deploy ordering and trigger:
# docs/TODO.md §Media Path Obligations, "Delete the retired `MH_MAX_STREAMS`".
MH_MAX_STREAMS=100
# TLS certificate paths for WebTransport (mounted from mh-service-tls Secret)
MH_TLS_CERT_PATH=/etc/mh-tls/tls.crt
MH_TLS_KEY_PATH=/etc/mh-tls/tls.key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# QUIC transport parameters (ADR-0036 §1)
#
# ADR-0036 §1 requires MH to declare these explicitly rather than inherit
# quinn's defaults: "a bound has to be declared to be assertable, and the
# default being adequate is not the same as the default being chosen."
#
# ANCHOR (DRY): `crates/mh-service/src/config.rs` currently supplies fallbacks
# for some of these via `unwrap_or`, which is a second encoding of the same
# quantities that `dt-guard env-config` cannot see (its check 1 keys off
# `ConfigError::MissingEnvVar`, which `unwrap_or` never emits). Task #12
# collapses each pair by making the vars required. Until then these comments
# are the only thing holding the two in agreement — an anchor, not a guard.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Ingress bound: how many unidirectional QUIC streams a PEER may have open
# toward MH at once. MH advertises this limit; QUIC enforces it by withholding
# stream credit, so the peer stalls rather than MH rejecting. Under ADR-0036
# §1's one-group-of-pictures-per-stream rule this bounds how many video groups
# from one publisher can be in flight. §1's working figure is ~5 concurrent
# (five video slots at a one-second keyframe cadence), and a publisher has
# roughly one group in flight per encoding — single digits even with simulcast.
# 64 leaves generous headroom while sitting below quinn's unchosen default of
# 100, so the ceiling is demonstrably ours.
# This does NOT bound MH's egress — the client's own advertised limit does.
# Story 1 is audio-only, so the operative count is 0; the bound is declared
# because a bound must be declared to be assertable.
# Enforced by: quinn, in MH's process, at stream-open time.
# Where an operator sees it: nowhere directly — a peer at the limit stalls
# silently. Diagnose from the publisher side, not from an MH metric.
MH_MAX_CONCURRENT_UNI_STREAMS=64

# Egress backstop: the QUIC datagram SEND buffer — the egress datagram queue
# toward a subscriber. It does NOT size the ingress buffer, which is a
# separate transport parameter declared in code (task #12); quinn's
# `datagram_receive_buffer_size` also does double duty as the source of the
# advertised `max_datagram_frame_size`, so the two directions have unrelated
# sizing pressures and must not share a number.
#
# Expressed in FRAMES of audio
# (20 ms of Opus each), not bytes — ADR-0036 §1 requires the frame form,
# because quinn's 1 MiB default is ~93 seconds of queued audio and "1 MiB"
# does not make that visible while "93 seconds" does. Code converts to bytes
# once at config load (quinn's API takes bytes).
#
# This is a PER-CONNECTION budget shared across every audio stream that
# connection carries. At the story-1 loopback shape (one stream) 32 frames is
# 640 ms; with N publishers multiplexed it is 640/N ms per stream.
#
# It is a BACKSTOP, never the operative queue. ADR-0036 §1 requires MH's
# application-level egress queue to trip FIRST so back-pressure is observable
# in our code rather than inside quinn; task #12 sets that bound to 8 frames
# per connection and validates the ordering at startup. Keep this value
# strictly ABOVE that bound or MH will refuse to start.
# Enforced by: quinn, in MH's process. Its overflow behaviour here is to
# silently discard the oldest queued datagram, and MH cannot see it happen.
#
# Where an operator sees what MH itself sheds, what each media signal cannot
# see, and why no alert may rest on the queue-depth gauge alone:
# `docs/observability/metrics/mh-service.md` §Media Forward Path. That file is
# authoritative for all of it and this comment does not restate it.
#
# CORRECTED 2026-09-05 (story task 16). This block previously said MH "uses
# the non-dropping send path, which returns a `Blocked` error when this buffer
# is full", and that this buffer's occupancy is published as a gauge. Both
# were false. The correction is recorded rather than quietly applied because
# an operator-facing comment that is wrong AND authoritative-sounding recruits
# reviewers into repeating it, and no guard reads manifest comments. Kept to
# the one clause that stops an operator re-deriving the false version:
#
#   THERE IS NO TRANSPORT ERROR TO COUNT, on either quinn path — the
#   non-blocking one treats the blocked arm as unreachable, and the
#   congestion-aware one expresses back-pressure by AWAITING rather than by
#   returning a value. MH's own bounded application queue is therefore the
#   ONLY observable shed, which is exactly why the startup validation requires
#   this value to sit strictly above it.
#
# Citations, quinn/wtransport versions and the accessor list live in
# `docs/TODO.md` §Media Path Obligations, which is the single home for this
# fact. Nothing here, in the catalog, on a dashboard or in a runbook may
# describe an MH egress signal as observing transport back-pressure.
MH_DATAGRAM_BUFFER_AUDIO_FRAMES=32

# QUIC connection-level keepalive, in milliseconds. This is what refreshes the
# NAT binding of a MUTED participant, who by definition sends no media
# (ADR-0036 §1, §5) — without it an intermediary can reap the path and unmute
# is not instantaneous. Load-bearing for this story's mute/unmute demo.
#
# Two relationships fix this value; it is meaningless in isolation:
#   - it must be well below the ~30 s reap window of an aggressive NAT, so at
#     least two refreshes fit inside one window (10 s gives three);
#   - it must be well below MH's effective QUIC idle timeout, since a
#     keepalive is only effective if it arrives before the peer times out.
#     Task #12 declares `max_idle_timeout` as a named 30 s constant, making
#     this a chosen 1:3 ratio rather than an inherited one. A 1:2 ratio would
#     put a single lost keepalive at the edge of the timeout.
# Enforced by: quinn, in MH's process.
# Where an operator sees it: as connection loss after a long mute. There is
# NO keepalive scenario in docs/runbooks/mh-incident-response.md today, and
# this comment deliberately names no scenario number.
#
# CORRECTED 2026-09-08. This block previously read "see
# mh-incident-response.md Scenario 15 (media datagram drop)" and justified it
# with "that runbook ends at Scenario 14 today; Scenario 15 is reserved for
# this story's operations runbook task." BOTH CLAIMS ARE NOW FALSE. The
# runbook does not end at 14, and the reserved slot was taken by an unrelated
# scenario — Scenario 15 is "Media Sessions Declining — No Sender Binding",
# which is a control-plane binding fault with no keepalive content at all. An
# operator diagnosing a post-mute connection loss who followed the old pointer
# landed in an MC-binding triage tree and would have paged the wrong service.
#
# The correction is recorded rather than quietly applied because the failure
# here was not the pointer being stale — it was RESERVING A NUMBER IN A
# SEQUENCE THIS FILE DOES NOT OWN. `dt-guard alert-rules-policy` validates
# that a `runbook_url` FILE exists but never resolves the `#anchor`, and
# nothing at all reads a manifest comment, so neither half of that pointer had
# a guard behind it. Do not re-introduce a number here when the keepalive
# scenario is eventually written; refer to it by TITLE, which survives
# renumbering, or link it from the runbook side where the guard can see it.
#
# Until that scenario exists, the diagnostic in one line: a connection lost
# after a long silent period, with no decline counted on
# `mh_media_session_starts_total` and no drop counted on
# `mh_media_frames_dropped_total`, is the idle-timeout/NAT-reap shape this
# value exists to prevent. It has no unique client-side discriminator — the
# client observes an ordinary connection close either way — so confirm from
# the MH side that the session was idle for the whole window before treating
# it as a keepalive fault.
MH_KEEPALIVE_INTERVAL_MS=10000

# Accept-time resource-exhaustion guard: the maximum number of concurrent
# WebTransport connections MH will accept. NEVER a capacity figure, never
# advertised to GC (what GC receives is the stream ceiling MH derives from
# `MH_EGRESS_BUDGET_BPS` below — a different quantity). It sits far above expected peak, so rejections
# attributed to it mean a connection flood or a connection leak, not demand
# that has outgrown the deployment.
#
# `500` is MEMORY-DERIVED against this pod's 1 Gi limit. MH declares its
# receive-side flow control explicitly in
# `crates/mh-service/src/webtransport/server.rs`, so the per-connection
# receive ceiling is bounded rather than unbounded:
#
#     receive_window               = 256 KiB  (the dominant term)
#     stream_receive_window        =  64 KiB  (bounded BY receive_window)
#     datagram_receive_buffer_size =   2 KiB
#   ≈ 264 KB per connection, so 500 is ~132 MB, ~13% of 1 Gi. The derivation
#   has one home — `crates/mh-service/src/config.rs`, at the constants
#   themselves — and this comment does not restate its reasoning.
#
# `datagram_receive_buffer_size` is its OWN constant and is deliberately NOT
# derived from MH_DATAGRAM_BUFFER_AUDIO_FRAMES: that quinn field also feeds
# the advertised max_datagram_frame_size, so the send and receive directions
# have opposing sizing pressures and must not share a number.
#
# CORRECTED 2026-09-22. This block previously said the three receive-side
# parameters were "all currently UNDECLARED by MH", put the per-connection
# adversarial ceiling at ~206 MB — "roughly five hostile connections, not
# 500" — and closed with "until then this bound is chosen, not derived." All
# of that described the tree before the transport-config work landed; every
# clause is now false. `server.rs` declares all three and asserts the
# rendered values (`receive_window: 262144`, `stream_receive_window: 65536`,
# `max_idle_timeout: Some(30000)`), and ADR-0036's Context note that MH has
# no `accept_uni` loop no longer holds either.
#
# The correction is recorded rather than quietly applied because an operator
# reading the old text would have concluded this pod tolerates about five
# connections — an order of magnitude below the truth — and throttled a
# healthy deployment on the strength of it. Nothing mechanical would have
# caught it: no guard reads a manifest comment, which is why this file
# carries its corrections in the open instead of rewriting them away.
#
# The memory figure is measured against this pod's 1 Gi limit — so changing
# `resources.limits.memory` in mh-{0,1}-deployment.yaml invalidates the
# arithmetic and requires revisiting this value.
# Enforced by: mh-service itself, in crates/mh-service/src/webtransport/server.rs,
# before allocating handler resources.
# Where an operator sees it: `mh_webtransport_connections_total{status="rejected"}`,
# alert `MHHighWebTransportRejections`, and
# docs/runbooks/mh-incident-response.md Scenario 5.
MH_MAX_CONNECTIONS=500

# ADR-0036 §11 media forward-path latency sampling: the fraction of forwarded
# frames whose latency is observed into `mh_media_forward_latency_seconds`.
#
# OPTIONAL. Absent, MH uses the code default
# (`mh_service::config::DEFAULT_MEDIA_LATENCY_SAMPLE_RATIO`), so removing this
# key does not stop a pod starting — deliberately, so the image can roll back
# alone. Present, it must parse and lie in 0.0..=1.0; MH refuses to start
# otherwise, because neither end fails safely: above 1.0 silently means
# "observe every frame" (a histogram write on the per-frame path) and below
# 0.0 silently means "observe none" (a histogram that is empty forever and
# reads as a healthy quiet path).
#
# Timestamps are taken on EVERY frame regardless — a monotonic clock read is
# ~25 ns and the ingress timestamp is needed for frame age anyway. This ratio
# governs only the histogram observation, which is the expensive part.
#
# The sampling is RANDOM PER FRAME. Do not "optimise" it into a modulo on a
# sequence number or a per-stream hash: those meet the same CPU budget and
# perfectly reconstruct which frames of one stream were observed, which is the
# voice-activity trace ADR-0036 §11 prohibits.
#
# NOT `OTEL_SAMPLE_RATE`, which is trace head-sampling with a different owner
# and a different consumer.
# Enforced by: mh-service, in crates/mh-service/src/config.rs at load.
# Where an operator sees it: the `mh_media_latency_sample_ratio` gauge, which
# publishes THIS SAME FIELD — not a second reading of it — so the published
# ratio cannot drift from the applied one.
#
# Setting it explicitly here is DELIBERATE even though it currently equals the
# code default. It is an operator tuning surface, `dashboard-conventions.md`
# cites this key as the authority for the deployed cadence, and an absent key
# gives a responder nothing to read. The trade is real and worth naming: while
# this line exists, a future change to the code default does NOT reach this
# cluster — the manifest wins. If the default is ever re-chosen, revisit this
# line rather than assuming it follows.
MH_MEDIA_LATENCY_SAMPLE_RATIO=0.01

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Egress-budget admission chain (story 2 R-19, R-23; ADR-0036 §11
# "Admission control is keyed on egress bandwidth, not connection count")
#
# THIS FILE IS THE SINGLE AUTHORITATIVE HOME FOR EVERY NUMBER IN THIS BLOCK,
# and it WINS ON DISAGREEMENT — but it is not the only place they appear.
# `.env.example` and the Media Handler block in `docs/DEVELOPMENT.md` mirror
# them for LOCAL RUNS and carry `ANCHOR (DRY)` pointers back here; changing a
# value here means updating those two. (They mirror the KIND OVERLAY's budget
# rather than this file's placeholder, deliberately — see
# `MH_EGRESS_BUDGET_BPS` below.) Neither mirror is a consumer: a drifted value
# there reaches a developer's laptop, never this cluster and never an alert.
#
# All six are REQUIRED keys with no Rust fallback, read by two different
# tasks: the four egress-chain keys (budget, both costs, threshold) by the
# MH egress-budget code task (story 2 task 8, landed),
# `MH_MAX_REGISTERED_MEETINGS` by the MH capacity-guard task (story 2 task
# 11, landed), and `MH_MAX_MUTED_SOURCES_PER_MEETING` by the MH server-mute
# task (story 2 task 10) — until that lands, the current image ignores that
# one. MH refuses to start when
# a key it reads is absent or malformed. That is deliberate — a default in
# code would be a second, invisible encoding of the same value, and
# `dt-guard env-config` can only see a key the code refuses to start
# without. The keys landed BEFORE the images that read them (the add-key
# half of the rollout-ordering rule).
#
# The chain: MH derives a STREAM ceiling (never "subscriber ceiling" — a
# subscriber now holds N slots) from the budget and the two per-kind costs,
# enforces it at stream admission, and advertises the same value to GC in
# the register RPC's existing `max_streams` field, where it replaces the
# retired `MH_MAX_STREAMS` above. GC placement against it stays SOFT; MH
# admission is the hard backstop. The derivation, and the refuse-to-boot
# floor under it, are code (`EgressAdmission` in
# crates/mh-service/src/config.rs) — they are deliberately not restated here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Egress bandwidth budget for this MH, in BITS per second.
#
# THIS VALUE IS A PLACEHOLDER AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT SIZED. It is a
# force-a-choice placeholder an operator must replace, not a capacity claim —
# in the same spirit as the `basis="unmeasured"` label on the budget gauge:
# ADR-0036 records that the egress budget has no measurement behind it and
# must fail closed, deliberately low. Because every stream is costed at the
# VIDEO rate (the ceiling divides by the larger of the two costs below, which
# keeps MH type-blind), this placeholder admits fewer streams than even one
# small audio-only meeting needs. That is intended. Do not file it as a bug;
# replace it.
#
# SET IT to this deployment's expected peak egress. No arithmetic appears
# here on purpose: this value is not sized, so there is no relationship to
# state and nothing for a comment to go stale against.
#
# It announces itself. MH (a) logs the effective derived stream ceiling
# loudly at startup, and (b) emits a distinct INFO "configure me" nudge
# naming THIS key whenever that ceiling is below MH's advisory recommended
# minimum (`mh_media_egress_stream_ceiling_recommended_min`). The nudge fires on this
# placeholder by design and goes quiet once a real budget is set. Read the
# startup log for the effective ceiling — do not compute it by hand.
#
# THE MARGIN, STATED WITHOUT NUMBERS: this placeholder still boots. It sits
# above MH's refuse-to-boot floor on the derived ceiling (a code constant,
# `MIN_EGRESS_STREAM_CEILING` in crates/mh-service/src/config.rs). The ONLY correct response to the startup nudge is
# a real budget here. NEVER lower that floor to quiet a nagging pod — the
# floor exists because a ceiling just above zero passes every validation,
# boots, gets placed, and then rejects nearly everything it is handed.
#
# CONSEQUENCE OF A STARVED BUDGET IS A NEW-MEETING OUTAGE PLUS SILENT MEDIA IN
# EXISTING MEETINGS, not a media degradation. The derived ceiling is advertised
# to GC; once `current_streams` reaches it, GC has no candidate handler and new
# meetings fail to place (ServiceUnavailable) — the same mechanism as scaling
# MH to zero. Joins into already-assigned meetings still succeed at GC (R-6)
# but are refused at admission and carry no media. (Corrected 2026-09-30 from
# "a join outage", which R-6 falsified.) Lowering this value is therefore NOT a
# kill switch or a load-shed lever (docs/runbooks/mh-deployment.md §Operator
# levers).
#
# The Kind dev cluster overrides this key (infra/kubernetes/overlays/kind/
# services/mh-service/configmap-egress-budget-patch.yaml), and that patch —
# not this file — carries the sizing formula and the requirement it was
# sized against.
# Enforced by: mh-service at stream admission (hard), and GC at placement
# (soft, new meetings only) — both from the ceiling derived from this value.
# Where an operator sees it: MH's startup log (effective ceiling and the
# configure-me nudge); the `mh_media_egress_budget_bytes_per_second
# {basis="unmeasured"}` and `mh_media_egress_stream_ceiling` gauges (live
# since story 2 task 8). MH converts bits to bytes exactly once at load, so
# the gauge is in BYTES while this key is in BITS by design.
MH_EGRESS_BUDGET_BPS=10000000

# Cost of ONE audio egress stream against the budget above, in BITS per
# second.
#
# PROVABLY INERT TODAY. The derived ceiling divides by the LARGER of this and
# `MH_STREAM_COST_VIDEO_BPS`, and video dominates by more than an order of
# magnitude; admission counts streams rather than byte-accounting per kind.
# So changing this value changes NOTHING an operator can observe — the key is
# not broken when tuning it moves no gauge. It becomes operative only when
# per-kind costing lands. It is required now so that change is a code edit,
# not a manifest rollout.
#
# PROVENANCE: the ADR-0036 §4 audio figure ("In-band key carriage", its
# "Cost, stated plainly" paragraph) — the ADR is the single source of truth
# for the cost model and this value mirrors it rather than re-deriving it.
# It is that §4 NOMINAL figure, NOT a measured or peak-bitrate wire cost.
#
# NOT a codec bitrate, and NOT any of these RELATED BUT DIFFERENT values —
# DO NOT UNIFY THEM:
#   - `MC_AUDIO_MAX_BITRATE_BPS` (infra/services/mc-service/config.env):
#     the MAXIMUM Opus bitrate MC directs a publisher to use;
#   - the supported audio bitrate floor and ceiling constants in
#     `crates/mh-service/src/config.rs`: the codec payload band MH assumes.
# This key is per-EGRESS-STREAM cost including per-packet, key-carriage and
# signature overhead, which is why it is larger than any of them.
#
# RESIDUAL COUPLING, recorded so it is not rediscovered: at MC's maximum
# directed bitrate the on-wire audio stream is heavier than this nominal
# figure, so this value UNDER-costs audio relative to what MC may direct.
# Non-operative while video dominates the ceiling (see above). When per-kind
# costing lands it becomes an OVER-ADMISSION bug unless reconciled, and the
# reconciliation is an ADR-0036 §4 decision (protocol + meeting-controller),
# not a ConfigMap edit. Tracked in docs/TODO.md §Media Path Obligations.
# Enforced by: mh-service, as an input to the derived stream ceiling.
# Where an operator sees it: nowhere directly — it cannot move the ceiling
# today. MH logs it at startup (`stream_cost_audio_bps`).
MH_STREAM_COST_AUDIO_BPS=90000

# Cost of ONE video egress stream against the budget above, in BITS per
# second.
#
# THIS IS THE OPERATIVE COST in story 2, even though story 2 sends only
# audio: the stream ceiling is derived against the LARGER of the two costs,
# which keeps MH type-blind (ADR-0036 §7) and means every admitted stream is
# costed as video. Raising this value LOWERS the ceiling; lowering it RAISES
# the ceiling and over-admits once video is real.
#
# CHOSEN, NOT MEASURED — a nominal single-stream video figure with no
# measurement behind it, carried under the same ADR-0036 open item as the
# budget. Revisit when video lands.
# Enforced by: mh-service, as the dominant input to the derived stream
# ceiling.
# Where an operator sees it: through its effect on
# `mh_media_egress_stream_ceiling`; MH logs it at startup
# (`stream_cost_video_bps`).
MH_STREAM_COST_VIDEO_BPS=2500000

# Rejection-ratio threshold for stream admission, 0.0 to 1.0.
#
# ONE VALUE, TWO CONSUMERS, ONE HOME. MH admission reads it, and the
# egress-exhaustion alert reads it — but the alert reads it ONLY through the
# `mh_media_stream_admission_rejection_ratio_threshold` gauge, which MH
# publishes from THIS SAME FIELD, not from a second reading of it (the
# `MH_MEDIA_LATENCY_SAMPLE_RATIO` precedent above). PromQL cannot read a
# ConfigMap, so the alert MUST compare gauge-to-gauge and MUST NEVER carry
# this number as a PromQL literal: a literal makes one configured value two
# encodings on the day it ships, and this file is the half nobody reads.
#
# CHOSEN, NOT DERIVED. MH has no ratified SLO target (docs/observability/
# slos.md carries it OPEN pending story 8), so this is a judgement about what
# is worth a business-hours look — warning-grade, never a page — with no
# measurement behind it. Revisit when the MH target ratifies. When the alert
# fires, investigate; do not tune this number to quiet it.
#
# Two properties of the published ratio this value is only meaningful
# against (both implemented by story 2 task 8 — the window is 5 minutes;
# see docs/observability/metrics/mh-service.md):
#   - The ratio is WINDOWED (a sliding window or the last N admission
#     decisions), never a lifetime ratio. A lifetime ratio is silent during
#     total exhaustion on a long-healthy pod and cannot clear on a fresh one.
#   - No admission decisions in the window publishes a value that does not
#     fire, but zero accepted with any rejected publishes 1.0, NEVER 0.0 —
#     otherwise the alert is quietest exactly when the failure is total.
# Enforced by: mh-service (a debugging-breadcrumb WARN when the windowed
# ratio crosses it), and MHMediaEgressBudgetExhausted via the threshold gauge.
# Where an operator sees it: `mh_media_stream_admission_rejection_ratio` and
# `..._threshold` (live since story 2 task 8); alert
# `MHMediaEgressBudgetExhausted` (mh-alerts.yaml; runbook MH Scenario 18).
MH_EGRESS_REJECTION_RATIO_THRESHOLD=0.05

# Resource-exhaustion guard: the maximum number of meeting registrations MH
# will hold. NEVER a capacity figure — not advertised to GC, not a placement
# input.
#
# Needed because the egress-edge bound below is structurally blind to one
# surface: an empty `egress_streams` set is legal and meaningful, so
# arbitrarily many near-free meetings would never trip it. This bounds that.
# It is defence-in-depth against a buggy or compromised MC inside the operator
# trust boundary (the registration contract authorizes on the
# `service.write.mh` scope alone), not against an anonymous caller.
#
# NOT A CONCURRENCY BOUND TODAY — it counts CUMULATIVELY over pod lifetime.
# `SessionState.registered_meetings` is insert-only: nothing removes an ended
# meeting's entry, because MH acts on no meeting-ended signal yet. So this
# bound is consumed by every DISTINCT meeting ever registered on this pod, at
# whatever rate meetings finish — independent of concurrent load, rising with
# uptime. Do NOT size it against expected concurrent meetings, and do not
# describe reaching it as a bug, a leak or a hostile MC: ordinary turnover
# reaches it. That framing was retracted once already for the sibling bound
# (docs/TODO.md §Media Path Obligations) and must not come back by this route.
# It stays cumulative EVEN AFTER R-20's teardown lands — unlike the sibling
# edge bound, this count is not reclaimed by teardown, because a resurrected
# empty meeting regrows it at ~0 edge cost. The mechanism and why 8192 does
# not move when R-20 merges are in the SIZING block below; this line flags
# only that the naive "R-20 fixes it" reading is wrong here.
#
# AN EMPTY MEETING IS NEAR-FREE TO CREATE BUT NOT FREE TO KEEP, and this is
# the half that is easy to miss. THE APPLY PATH IS O(REGISTERED MEETINGS):
# `RoutingTable::install` is the only apply path and it calls
# `RoutingSnapshot::with_policy` (crates/mh-service/src/routing/mod.rs), which
# clones the whole meetings map on EVERY apply. So a retained entry is O(1) to
# create and then permanently taxes every subsequent real registration. This
# key therefore sizes a PER-APPLY CPU COST, not only memory — and it becomes
# O(M^2) steady-state once ADR-0036 §8's periodic re-assert lands (story 4),
# because M meetings each re-asserting costs O(M) per apply.
#
# DO NOT REASON ABOUT THIS KEY FROM ITS MEMORY FOOTPRINT. A retained entry is
# small in bytes and costs ~0 edges, so "each slot is near-free, we can afford
# many more" is arithmetically true and operationally wrong — it is the exact
# reasoning that would raise this value back up, and the apply-cost axis is
# the one that binds.
#
# SIZING — 8192, and why it is not the larger value first proposed. Two owners
# weighed in at review and both rulings turn on whether R-20's teardown has
# landed:
#   - @media-handler: size it so the EDGE bound binds first for any meeting
#     carrying real edges, leaving this key to catch only the empty/near-free
#     flood the edge bound is structurally blind to. That argues for
#     `MH_MAX_TOTAL_EGRESS_EDGES / 2`.
#   - @security: that larger value is approved ONLY IF R-20 lands, because
#     without reclamation the retained entries are cumulative and each one
#     taxes every apply (above). Absent R-20: 8192, knowingly preempting the
#     edge bound.
# 8192 IS UNCONDITIONAL — it is not a hedge against R-20 slipping. Both
# owners' conditions resolve here for the same reason: the bound is cumulative
# whether or not teardown lands (see the paragraph below), so it is correct on
# the availability axis AND the security axis as the tree stands and as it
# will stand after R-20. The sibling edge bound makes the same choice about
# R-20 deliberately; sizing this key on an assumption its sibling refuses
# would be an internal inconsistency, and a value whose safety depends on an
# unlanded task is a masked contingency rather than a chosen bound.
#
# CONSEQUENCE, STATED PLAINLY: at 8192 this bound is DELIBERATELY TIGHTER in
# meeting-equivalents than `MH_MAX_TOTAL_EGRESS_EDGES`, so for small meetings
# it PREEMPTS the edge bound — it trips first, not only on an empty-meeting
# flood, and it is therefore the binding availability bound for small-meeting
# load until reclamation is genuinely closed (fence + resurrection — see
# below), not merely until R-20 lands. Chosen knowingly: a capped apply-cost tax is preferable to
# an unbounded quadratic one with no saturation signal behind it.
#
# R-20 LANDING DOES NOT MAKE THIS BOUND CONCURRENT — do not raise this value
# on the day teardown merges. `EndMeeting` carries no generation by design, so
# a `RegisterMeeting` arriving after a teardown RE-CREATES the meeting;
# reclamation therefore rests on an MC-side quiesce that MH does not enforce,
# and the caller it rests on is exactly the principal this key is
# defence-in-depth against. Teardown-then-resurrect regrows the map without
# ever tripping the edge bound, because a resurrected empty meeting is still
# ~0 edges. So the bound stays effectively cumulative even after R-20 and 8192
# stands. Fuller reasoning lives in docs/TODO.md §Media Path Obligations (the
# sibling unreclaimed-routing-entry defect, and the `EndMeeting`-has-no-fence
# entry); this comment cites them and restates neither. The honest revisit
# trigger is "when the fence and resurrection questions are CLOSED", not "when
# R-20 lands".
#
# Hard ceiling: validated against `MAX_REGISTERED_MEETINGS_CEILING` in
# `crates/mh-service/src/config.rs`, on the same footing as the four §8
# bounds below (a `> 0` check alone would let a fat-fingered value silently
# re-open the surface this key exists to close), and 0 is refused.
# Enforced by: mh-service, refusing a NEW meeting id loudly (RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED)
# and counting it by its own reason,
# `mh_media_policy_applies_total{outcome="rejected_meeting_cap"}`. A
# re-registration of a held meeting is never refused.
# Where an operator sees it: `mh_media_registered_meetings` against
# `mh_media_registered_meetings_limit`, and that rejection outcome. READ THE
# GAUGE AS REGISTRATIONS HELD, NOT MEETINGS IN PROGRESS: it falls only when an
# `EndMeeting` releases a meeting, so a meeting whose MC never COMPLETES it
# (an MC crashed or killed before or during teardown; retries exhausted or an
# MH predating the RPC; a rollback to an MC build without teardown) stays
# counted until the pod restarts — reading it as concurrency understates
# exhaustion in the direction that hides it.
MH_MAX_REGISTERED_MEETINGS=8192

# Resource-exhaustion guard, §8-guard family: the maximum size of
# `RegisterMeetingRequest.server_muted_sources`, a caller-controlled repeated
# field. A PRE-ALLOCATION bound — MH rejects an over-limit registration
# BEFORE any per-element work or routing-table build, the same posture as the
# egress-stream and candidate-source bounds below. NEVER a capacity figure.
#
# WHY IT IS NOT SUBSUMED BY THE EGRESS-STREAM BOUND: a hostile snapshot can
# carry zero egress streams and a very large muted set. This key is the only
# thing sizing the muted set's allocation.
#
# SIZING, and its precondition. Its number is taken from
# `MH_MAX_EGRESS_STREAMS_PER_MEETING` below. The muted set rides ONE handler's
# snapshot, and the per-handler filter is "muted senders that SEND TO this
# handler" — senders owning at least one edge on it (story 2 task 20's edge
# model; implemented by the MC server-mute task). Each such sender is the
# pinned source of at least one of this handler's egress streams, so the
# invariant is: muted senders on a handler <= distinct sources on it <= its
# egress streams <= the egress-stream bound. Under that filter a legitimate
# snapshot can never name more muted senders than the egress bound admits,
# which makes this a LOOSE bound that can never falsely reject a legitimate
# snapshot — NOT a statement that a meeting can hold this many senders.
# Until that filter exists, an UNFILTERED set is bounded only by the
# meeting's muted count, and the never-falsely-rejects claim needs that
# count to stay within this value.
#
# CORRECTION (story 2 task 20). The invariant above previously read "muted
# senders on a handler <= P_h, the participants PLACED ON this handler" —
# task 6's one-handler-per-participant placement, which task 20 replaced.
# Participants are no longer placed on a handler; edges are. The per-sender
# form above needs no slot-declaration precondition: a sender that sends to
# the handler holds a stream there by definition.
#
# CORRECTION (story 2 task 6). This paragraph previously rested on "every
# sender is also a subscriber holding at least one egress stream". That was
# true only because story 1's visibility predicate was REFLEXIVE — the
# self-edge guaranteed every sender a stream. Story 2 R-3 removes loopback,
# so it is no longer true (see the PRECONDITION below). Practical impact is
# nil: a false reject needs more than 512 muted senders on one handler.
#
# THE DIRECTION IS LOAD-BEARING: this value must be AT LEAST
# `MH_MAX_EGRESS_STREAMS_PER_MEETING`. The two need not be equal, but lowering
# this one below that bound breaks the never-falsely-rejects claim above —
# a legitimate snapshot naming more muted senders than this admits is then
# rejected WHOLE, so server mute silently stops working for large meetings and
# the symptom is a rejected registration, which is not mute-shaped. Asserted,
# not merely stated, by
# crates/env-tests/tests/01_mh_deployment_config.rs::test_story2_keys_wired_into_every_mh_instance.
#
# WHY THE TWO ARE EQUAL TODAY, and why that is an answer rather than a
# coincidence: two pressures meet exactly here. Security wants this value as
# SMALL as possible, because it sizes an allocation against a caller-controlled
# count. Correctness wants it AT LEAST the egress bound, or legitimate
# registrations are rejected. 512 is the tightest value satisfying both — so a
# future change to either key has to move both.
#
# PRECONDITION: the derivation holds only while the muted set MC sends a
# handler is filtered to senders that SEND TO that handler (own at least one
# edge on it). The invariant counts senders by the streams they SOURCE on the
# handler, and such a sender sources one by definition; a muted sender that
# sends nowhere on this handler is filtered out and costs nothing here.
#   What still breaks it: an UNFILTERED set (the state until the MC
#   server-mute task lands), or a filter by any other rule — the task that
#   implements server mute MUST filter by edge ownership, not by
#   connectivity: a sender CONNECTED to a handler need not send to it.
#   What no longer breaks it (story 2 task 20): the three zero-stream
#   participant cases listed here before — a joined-but-undeclared
#   participant, a participant with no peer on its handler, and a zero-slot /
#   send-only participant. Each holds zero streams as a SUBSCRIBER, which
#   mattered only while the invariant counted participants placed on the
#   handler; it now counts sources.
#
# REQUIREMENT ON THE MH CODE TASK, recorded here because this comment is what
# reaches that task's author: this required read must be validated against a
# hard-ceiling constant in `crates/mh-service/src/config.rs`, on the same
# footing as the four §8 bounds below. The ceiling is what keeps this a
# PRE-ALLOCATION guard rather than an operator-settable allocation size:
# raised to a huge value by a fat-fingered edit or a well-meaning "raise the
# limit" during an incident, this is not a loosened guard but NO guard — MH
# pre-allocates against an attacker-supplied count again, the precise
# condition this key exists to foreclose. A `> 0` check alone does not do it.
# Enforced by: mh-service (story 2 task 10), in the policy-limit checks, before
# any per-element work or routing-table build; the hard ceiling is
# `MAX_MUTED_SOURCES_PER_MEETING_CEILING` in `crates/mh-service/src/config.rs`,
# and MH REFUSES TO BOOT if this value is below
# MH_MAX_EGRESS_STREAMS_PER_MEETING (`ConfigError::MutedSourceBoundBelowEgressBound`).
# Where an operator sees it: an over-limit (or duplicate-sender) registration
# surfaces like the other pre-allocation bounds below —
# `mh_media_policy_applies_total{outcome="rejected_invalid"}` plus the WARN's
# `reason`; the enforced mute itself is `mh_media_frames_dropped_total{reason="server_muted"}`.
MH_MAX_MUTED_SOURCES_PER_MEETING=512

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADR-0036 §8 forwarding-policy bounds (story 2 R-22)
#
# ALL FOUR ARE RESOURCE-EXHAUSTION GUARDS, NEVER CAPACITY FIGURES. None is
# advertised to GC and none participates in placement. They bound what one
# `RegisterMeetingRequest` — and the sum of them — may make MH allocate.
#
# REQUIRED READS since story 2 task 8, with NO code default: this file is
# the single home for each number, and `dt-guard env-config` rule 1 now sees
# all four (it keys off `ConfigError::MissingEnvVar`). The values were chosen
# equal to the former code defaults, so behaviour did not change at cutover.
#
# Each key also has a HARD CEILING constant in
# `crates/mh-service/src/config.rs`, because a required read still needs its
# upper bound. The notes below cite that ceiling by ROLE, not by symbol name,
# so they stay true through any rename — grep the env var name in config.rs
# and you land on it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Per-meeting bound on `RegisterMeetingRequest.egress_streams`.
#
# WHAT BINDS SINCE STORY 2 (static join-order slot fill): each registration
# carries ONE egress stream per FILLED receive slot, each with exactly one
# pinned source, so this is now the binding per-meeting constraint — and it
# is PER HANDLER: the count is the number of edges THIS handler OWNS — one
# per filled receive slot whose edge MC assigned here. SIZE IT AGAINST THE
# WHOLE MEETING, not a share of it: MC co-locates edges (fewest send targets
# per sender), so in a meeting where everyone is connected to every handler
# ALL of its edges land on ONE handler. The worst case per handler is
# therefore the meeting's sum over subscribers of min(N_s, P - 1), where N_s
# is that subscriber's declared slots and P the meeting's participants; with
# a uniform N that is P x min(N, P - 1). With `MC_MAX_RECEIVE_SLOTS` = 8 that
# reaches this value at P = 64; the egress-budget admission ceiling above
# binds far earlier, so this remains a structural backstop, not the limit an
# operator meets. (Story 1's one-stream-per-subscriber-with-every-source
# shape made the candidate bound below the binding one instead.)
#
# CORRECTION (story 2 task 20). This note previously sized against P_h, "the
# participants placed on this handler", and said round-robin placement halves
# P per handler so meeting-P sizing over-provisions. Task 20 replaced
# one-handler-per-participant placement with the shared-handler edge model,
# and co-location concentrates rather than splits: the halving argument is
# false in the UNSAFE direction. No value changed.
#
# The muted-source bound above takes its number from this key, and the
# direction matters: its "can never falsely reject a legitimate snapshot"
# claim holds only while MH_MAX_MUTED_SOURCES_PER_MEETING >= this value.
# RAISING this value above it BREAKS that claim — a legitimate snapshot
# naming more muted senders than the muted bound admits is then rejected, and
# server mute silently stops working for large meetings. Lowering this value
# is safe (the muted bound merely becomes a looser pre-allocation guard).
# Asserted, not merely stated, by
# crates/env-tests/tests/01_mh_deployment_config.rs::test_story2_keys_wired_into_every_mh_instance.
# Code-side home: the hard-ceiling constant for
# `MH_MAX_EGRESS_STREAMS_PER_MEETING` in `crates/mh-service/src/config.rs`.
# Enforced by: mh-service, rejecting the WHOLE registration before building
# any routing table (live policy for that meeting is left unchanged).
# Where an operator sees it: `mh_media_policy_applies_total{outcome="rejected_invalid"}`,
# plus a WARN on target `mh.grpc.service` whose `reason` field names the bound
# that tripped — the counter alone does not say which bound.
MH_MAX_EGRESS_STREAMS_PER_MEETING=512

# Per-egress-stream bound on `EgressStream.candidate_sources`.
#
# NO LONGER BINDS under story 2's static fill: every stream carries exactly
# one MC-pinned source. It REBINDS when story 5's MH-side §7 selection
# returns multi-candidate streams, so do not lower it to 1 because today's
# traffic never exceeds 1.
#
# Code-side home: the hard-ceiling constant for
# `MH_MAX_CANDIDATE_SOURCES_PER_EGRESS` in `crates/mh-service/src/config.rs`.
# Enforced by: mh-service, rejecting the WHOLE registration before building
# any routing table.
# Where an operator sees it: as for the egress-stream bound above — the
# `rejected_invalid` outcome plus the WARN's `reason` field.
MH_MAX_CANDIDATE_SOURCES_PER_EGRESS=16

# Aggregate bound on egress edges across ALL meetings on this MH.
#
# READ THE CODE BEFORE SIZING THIS. What this bound actually measures, why
# raising it is not a remedy, and why an earlier framing of it was retracted
# are stated once in `crates/mh-service/src/config.rs` and once in the
# docs/TODO.md §Media Path Obligations entry on MH never reclaiming an ended
# meeting's routing entry. This comment cites them and restates neither.
#
# WHERE IN config.rs: the docstring of the hard-ceiling constant for
# `MH_MAX_TOTAL_EGRESS_EDGES`. Story 2 task 8 deleted the former code default
# when it made this read required, and MOVED that prose — intact,
# retraction included — to the ceiling constant, which survives.
# For the symptom as a responder meets it, see the reclamation note inside
# docs/runbooks/mh-incident-response.md, "Scenario 13: RegisterMeeting
# Timeout — Clients Kicked" ("Some people can't hear anything in a meeting
# that's otherwise working").
#
# MH implements the R-20 teardown path (`EndMeeting` reclaims an ended
# meeting's edges), but that makes this an ordinary resource guard only for
# meetings whose MC COMPLETES it: MC calls it for every meeting it ends as of
# story 2 task 12, and a meeting whose MC never completes it (crash, kill
# mid-teardown, exhausted retries, rollback) still holds its edges until the
# pod restarts. See the citations above.
# Code-side home: the hard-ceiling constant for
# `MH_MAX_TOTAL_EGRESS_EDGES` in `crates/mh-service/src/config.rs`.
# Enforced by: mh-service, when a registration would ADD edges — as a
# DEFENSE-IN-DEPTH BACKSTOP since story 2 task 8: the derived egress stream
# ceiling (same unit) is checked FIRST, and MH refuses to start when that
# ceiling exceeds this value, so this bound must stay AT OR ABOVE the ceiling.
# Where an operator sees it: the saturation signal is `mh_media_egress_edges`
# (installed streams, live since story 2 task 8) against
# `mh_media_egress_stream_ceiling` — the ceiling binds first, as
# `rejected_stream_ceiling`. If this backstop ever trips it shows as
# `mh_media_policy_applies_total{outcome="apply_failed"}` plus the WARN's
# `reason`. `mh_media_egress_edges_limit` publishes this value (the same field
# enforcement reads). `MHMediaEgressEdgeHeadroomLow` (mh-alerts.yaml) warns on
# edges approaching the stream ceiling, which binds first.
MH_MAX_TOTAL_EGRESS_EDGES=65536

# Bound on awaiting the session actor's config-apply reply, in milliseconds.
#
# The apply is asynchronous relative to the RPC response (ADR-0036 §8), so a
# wedged actor must produce a truthful stale `applied_generation` with
# `outcome=apply_failed`, never a hung RPC.
# Code-side home: the hard-ceiling constant for
# `MH_POLICY_APPLY_TIMEOUT_MS` in `crates/mh-service/src/config.rs`.
# Enforced by: mh-service, on the RegisterMeeting path.
# Where an operator sees it: `mh_media_policy_applies_total{outcome="apply_failed"}`.
MH_POLICY_APPLY_TIMEOUT_MS=1000
